  "entry_kind": "person",
  "entry_type": "Individual thinker or historian",
  "scope_note": "This entry presents the agricultural-crisis and mentalities explanation of Lower Canada and Ouellet's later account of Quebec historiography. Read on 2026-10-14: the 1962 article with Hamelin (pp. 17–33), his 1976 reply to his critics (pp. 115–132) and his 1985 survey (pp. 11–29, 38, 47, 51–52, 64–68), all in full or in the parts named, from Érudit page images. His books (1966, 1972, 1976) are unread; their words appear only as Paquet and Wallot (1972) and Tousignant (1980) quote them, labelled so. He is not presented as the founder of social history in Quebec: Bouchard (1990, ¶13) says Ouellet and Hamelin 'avec quelques autres, ont introduit ici l'histoire sociale'. Le Goff's review of the controversy (1974) and Paquet and Wallot's CHR reply (1975) are unread; Ouellet reports that Le Goff concluded for a crisis 'dès le début du XIXe siècle'. Greer's Patriots and the People (1993) is unread; its relation to Ouellet is given in reviewers' words (Dessureault 1998, Cardinal 1999), not as Greer's. Ouellet's 1995 review essay on Greer (Histoire sociale) could not be reached. Judgements on French Canadians, the Patriotes and nationalism quoted here are his claims, not adopted."
 },
