{
  "id": "quebec_historiography",
  "label": "Quebec historiography",
  "date_label": "Earlier national history (Groulx) · Institut d’histoire de l’Amérique française founded 1946, its Revue from 1947 · Montreal and Laval schools 1950s–60s · Histoire du Québec contemporain 1979 · Rudin’s critique 1992 · selected later readings (Catholic Action revision 2002–05)",
  "layer": "historiographical_developments",
  "period": "period_1",
  "hunt_core_paradigm": false,
  "description": "Historical writing on Quebec and French Canada, mostly in French, long organized around the nation, the Conquest of 1760 and the meaning of modernity. Lionel Groulx’s national history of French Canada closes what a francophone anthology calls the time of the ‘ancients’; Groulx founded the Institut d’histoire de l’Amérique française (1946) and its Revue (1947). In the 1950s historians at Montréal (Séguin, Frégault, Brunet) made the Conquest and its consequences their central problem. At Laval, Trudel wrote the history of New France, and Hamelin and Ouellet economic and social history, Ouellet in a conjunctural idiom indebted to Labrousse. Their agricultural-crisis thesis (1962) was contested by Paquet and Wallot (1972–73); Dechêne, trained in the Annales milieu in France, wrote the social history of seventeenth-century Montreal (1974). After 1970 a modernist generation turned to contemporary, urban and industrial Quebec and to its whole population (Linteau, Durocher and Robert, 1979). Women’s history followed, from the Collectif Clio (1982) to Baillargeon (1991); Lévesque (1997) asked how far colleagues had integrated it. Behiels (1985) found the Quiet Revolution’s prelude in 1945–60; Rouillard (1997) recorded a shift from reading it as rupture to turn. Ronald Rudin (1992, 1997) argued that the modernists’ search for a ‘normal’ society muted religion, rurality and ethnicity; Linteau and Fecteau replied (1995). Bouchard and Lamonde (2000) close the period; after 2000, Meunier and Warren, Bienvenue and Gauvreau traced the Quiet Revolution to Catholic Action.",
  "representative_figures_and_works": "Groulx, Histoire du Canada français depuis la découverte (1950); IHAF (1946) and RHAF (1947); Séguin, La “nation canadienne” et l’agriculture (thesis 1947; 1970); Frégault, La guerre de la Conquête (1955); Brunet, La présence anglaise et les Canadiens (1958); Trudel, L’esclavage au Canada français (1960) and Histoire de la Nouvelle-France (1963–); Hamelin, Économie et société en Nouvelle-France (1960); Ouellet and Hamelin, “La crise agricole dans le Bas-Canada” (1962); Ouellet, Histoire économique et sociale du Québec, 1760–1850 (1966); Paquet and Wallot (1972–73); Wallot, Un Québec qui bougeait (1973); Dechêne, Habitants et marchands (1974); Savard (1974); Gagnon, Le Québec et ses historiens (1978); Linteau, Durocher and Robert, Histoire du Québec contemporain (1979; 1986); Collectif Clio (1982); Linteau, “La nouvelle histoire du Québec vue de l’intérieur” (1983); Behiels, Prelude to Quebec’s Quiet Revolution (1985); Lévesque, La norme et les déviantes (1989) and “Réflexions sur l’histoire des femmes dans l’histoire du Québec” (1997); Baillargeon, Ménagères au temps de la Crise (1991); Rudin, “Revisionism and the Search for a Normal Society” (1992; French 1995) and Making History in Twentieth-Century Quebec (1997); Linteau and Fecteau (1995); Dumont, Genèse de la société québécoise (1993); Dickinson and Young, A Short History of Quebec (1993 ed.); Bouchard (1996, 2000); Martel (1997); Rouillard, “La Révolution tranquille: rupture ou tournant?” (1997); Lamonde (2000). Later: Meunier and Warren, Sortir de la « grande noirceur » (2002); Bienvenue, Quand la jeunesse entre en scène (2003); Gauvreau, The Catholic Origins of Quebec’s Quiet Revolution (2005); Bédard and Goyette, eds., Parole d’historiens (2006); Baillargeon, Brève histoire des femmes au Québec (2012).",
