{"id": "qcreq_rudin_1995", "title": "Ronald Rudin, “La quête d’une société normale: critique de la réinterprétation de l’histoire du Québec,” Bulletin d’histoire politique 3, no. 2 (1995): 9–42; reprinted in Éric Bédard and Julien Goyette, eds., Parole d’historiens: anthologie des réflexions sur l’histoire du Québec (Montréal: Presses de l’Université de Montréal, 2006), OpenEdition, chap. 43.", "url": "https://books.openedition.org/pum/13243", "citation": "Ronald Rudin, “La quête d’une société normale: critique de la réinterprétation de l’histoire du Québec,” Bulletin d’histoire politique 3, no. 2 (1995): 9–42; reprinted in Éric Bédard and Julien Goyette, eds., Parole d’historiens: anthologie des réflexions sur l’histoire du Québec (Montréal: Presses de l’Université de Montréal, 2006), OpenEdition, chap. 43.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-12", "note": "Read the opening and summary: ‘révisionnistes’ of the 1970s portrayed Quebec as developing like other Western societies, stressing urbanization, industrialization and class and downplaying ethnic, linguistic, religious and rural distinctiveness; a needed correction of the nationalist narrative that swung too far; a call for a ‘post-révisionniste’ balance. Names Linteau, Robert, Rouillard, Normand Séguin, Durocher, Young, Greer and Dickinson among the revisionists, and Ouellet as a critic of them. Checked against: Crossref DOI record (BHP 3:2, first page 9); OpenEdition reprint with editors’ source note (pp. 9–42)."}, "scope_note": "The French version of the critique and its list of ‘revisionists’."}
{"id": "qcreq_savard_1974", "title": "Pierre Savard, “Un quart de siècle d’historiographie québécoise, 1947–1972,” Recherches sociographiques 15, no. 1 (1974): 77–96; excerpt reprinted in Éric Bédard and Julien Goyette, eds., Parole d’historiens: anthologie des réflexions sur l’histoire du Québec (Montréal: Presses de l’Université de Montréal, 2006), OpenEdition, chap. 40.", "url": "https://books.openedition.org/pum/13238", "citation": "Pierre Savard, “Un quart de siècle d’historiographie québécoise, 1947–1972,” Recherches sociographiques 15, no. 1 (1974): 77–96; excerpt reprinted in Éric Bédard and Julien Goyette, eds., Parole d’historiens: anthologie des réflexions sur l’histoire du Québec (Montréal: Presses de l’Université de Montréal, 2006), OpenEdition, chap. 40.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-12", "note": "Excerpt read: the period saw a ‘rationalisation’ of history; Séguin, Frégault and Brunet share an ideological outlook; Brunet long wanted to guide the nation; Trudel and Ouellet are usually grouped as the ‘École de Québec’ though Savard treats them as distinct; Ouellet owed a debt to Labrousse and Chaunu; historians became professional academics. The excerpt does not discuss the RHAF’s founding or name the Annales. Checked against: Crossref DOI record (Recherches sociographiques 15:1, pp. 77–96); OpenEdition text of the reprinted excerpt."}, "scope_note": "A contemporary account of the Montreal and Laval schools and of Ouellet’s Labroussian economic history."}
{"id": "pe135_creighton_ouellet_1984", "title": "Fernand Ouellet, “Les classes dominantes au Québec, 1760–1840. Bilan historiographique,” Revue d’histoire de l’Amérique française 38, no. 2 (1984): 223–243.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.7202/304261ar", "citation": "Fernand Ouellet, “Les classes dominantes au Québec, 1760–1840. Bilan historiographique,” Revue d’histoire de l’Amérique française 38, no. 2 (1984): 223–243. doi:10.7202/304261ar.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Pp. 225–229 read (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 15 August 2022, text layer); the rest unread. Quoted: Creighton, 'partant de la théorie des staples pour établir les bases économiques du développement de la nation canadienne', held that New France's economy rested on the fur trade and was led by a bourgeoisie (p. 226, n. 13 citing the 1937 and 1944 books); 'le chef de l'Ecole laurentienne'; his post-Conquest French Canada 'une société semi-féodale décadente devenue hostile aux impératifs économiques'; Ouellet asks whether the later scheme of Séguin, Frégault and Brunet is 'bien différent' (p. 227)."}, "scope_note": "A Quebec historiographical survey placing Creighton's Conquest argument before the Montreal school's."}

