s two factors, one demographic, the other cultural\' and \'the atavistic mentality of the French-Canadian habitants led to their irrational refusal to accept technical improvements and other "reforms"\' (p. 213; n. 41 cites Ouellet, Lower Canada 1791–1840, 1980); \'The case of Sorel seems to challenge both elements of this interpretation\'; poverty arose \'primarily through the introduction of a certain form of capitalism\'; blaming the habitants \'would be adding ideological insult to economic injury\' (p. 214); n. 42 (p. 214) cites Normand Séguin\'s Hébertville study.'})}
